text:
Ben (support): FYI customer reported streaming silence after heartbeat then a reconnect that causes duplicate tokens and truncated JSON. Happens in our Python + TS SDKs. Client sees long silence (~6s) then reconnect, final chunk missing.

Jordan (customer): repro attached, small app using our Python iterator. After first 5s of tokens no further tokens, connection stays open, then client reconnects and we get a partial final message and double billing.

Ben (support): Can you paste the minimal repro code? customer included two snippets, shortened below.

Jordan (customer): Python repro:
```python
# simplified
from redwood import StreamingClient
client = StreamingClient(api_key=TOKEN)
it = client.stream("gpt-test", prompt="generate long list", stream=True)
for chunk in it:
    print(chunk.text, end="")
# hangs after ~5s, then on reconnect we get truncated JSON and duplicate tokens billed
```

Jordan (customer): TS repro (browser):
```ts
const es = new EventSource('/v1/stream?model=gpt-test', { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}` } });
es.onmessage = e => { /* parse chunk */ };
es.onerror = () => { /* reconnect logic fires */ };
// observe silence then reconnect
```

Alice (eng-runtime): Thanks — can you share request_id and approximate time window? Also which SDK versions? Might be related to heartbeat alignment patch we shipped last week.

Ben (support): request_id=rid_0X9aZ, window ~3567890100-3567890200, Python SDK 1.3.2, TS SDK 2.0.1. Customer says disabling client reconnect prevents duplicate billing but app still gets truncated final frame.

Carlos (eng): Re: heartbeat: we changed heartbeat padding logic to avoid spurious small frames. If client expects periodic small frames and triggers reconnect on >3s gap, it could close and re-open mid-stream. That would explain truncation and dup billing. Need to check server side reorder and resume semantics.

Tim (eng-ml): Also curious if KV-cache flush / finalization runs on connection close. If reconnect arrives with stale continuation token we might replay last tokens. Could cause dup billing.

Sara (customer-success): Customer is on a tight SLA, high token spend. Can we provide a mitigation to avoid duplicates while we triage?

Alice (eng-runtime): Short-term mitigation: ask client to increase idle timeout to 10s and disable aggressive reconnects; also set header `x-rw-streaming-mode: resume-v2` if available. We'll validate server-side fallback.

Ben (support): Sent mitigation to customer. They'd like an engineered repro we can run internally. Any lightweight script I can run to reproduce quickly?

Carlos (eng): I'll try to repro locally with a controlled heartbeat injector. Quick shell reproducer for Node (uses our TS SDK):
```js
const { StreamClient } = require('@redwood/sdk');
const c = new StreamClient({ key: process.env.T });
(async ()=>{
  const s = await c.stream({ model: 'gpt-test', stream: true });
  for await (const chunk of s) {
    process.stdout.write(chunk.delta || '');
    // artificially pause to trigger heartbeat gap
    if (Math.random() < 0.02) await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, 7000));
  }
})();
```

Tim (eng-ml): Good. I'll run that against prod-like staging and check for: missing final JSON closing token, duplicate token ranges, and whether `request_resume` header is generated.

incident-bot: Escalation created: #INC-2026, assigned to eng-runtime.

Alice (eng-runtime): I'll pick this up — rolling back heartbeat-padding in a canary on staging to compare. Will report back with timeline.

Ben (support): Thanks team — keeping customer posted. If anyone hits a repro please paste logs here with rid_0X9aZ.

Carlos (eng): one odd note: logs show a 100ms late ACK from edge node around 3567890125 which coincides with customer silence. Might be a kernel scheduling burst on the host. Still investigating.

Ben (support): Customer responded: "increased timeout helps, but want a fix", asked for ETA. I told them we'll follow up after engineering triage. :thumbsup:

Alice (eng-runtime): Will update by EOD with canary results.

Tim (eng-ml): If we confirm it's heartbeat padding, patch will be small (revert + improve unit tests). If it's host scheduling, we may need routing fallback.

Ben (support): Closing loop: customer appreciative of mitigation. Will await eng update and then schedule a postmortem if fix deploy required.
